WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:04.800
B.C. Local-Government Finance. Is each municipality keeping up with its infrastructure, and what would closing the gap cost in tax and borrowing?

2
00:00:05.250 --> 00:00:11.800
90 official Province of B.C. files, each held to a written contract, because they change shape from year to year.

3
00:00:12.250 --> 00:00:19.800
A dbt model on DuckDB turns the filings into indicators, with 31 data tests. All pass.

4
00:00:20.250 --> 00:00:27.800
68 municipalities invest less than their assets wear out. Together they are $570M a year short.

5
00:00:28.250 --> 00:00:35.300
The Province's own data has defects: its renewal-need field puts Vancouver at 41% of replacement value a year. The pipeline screens it out and re-derives the need.

6
00:00:35.750 --> 00:00:42.800
An Excel model a finance team would use: pick any of 161 municipalities and every sheet follows.

7
00:00:43.250 --> 00:00:50.300
For Fernie, closing the renewal gap by 2029 needs municipal tax increases averaging 3.7% a year.

8
00:00:50.750 --> 00:00:57.800
The workbook proves its own numbers against dbt: all 17 checks pass, and CI rebuilds the Gold layer byte for byte.

9
00:00:58.250 --> 00:01:04.300
Download the workbook, or read the ten-page board pack. Province of B.C. Local Government Statistics, Open Government Licence – British Columbia. Not affiliated with any municipality or the Province.

